::CW_U02_PRE_Q01::Passage: "I kept the ticket after the concert was canceled, pressing its bent corner flat every morning." What does the ticket most likely reveal?{
~A. The narrator dislikes music.#Incorrect. Keeping the ticket suggests attachment, not dislike.
=B. The narrator has an unresolved feeling connected to the canceled concert.#Correct. The repeated action shows the ticket carries emotional importance.
~C. The concert happened exactly as planned.#Incorrect. The passage says it was canceled.
~D. The ticket has no role in the scene.#Incorrect. The ticket is the central object.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q02::Passage: "You stand outside the office, rehearsing the truth until every version sounds like a lie." What point of view is used?{
~A. Third-person omniscient.#Incorrect. The passage does not use he, she, or they with access to many minds.
~B. First-person plural.#Incorrect. First-person plural uses "we."
~C. Objective camera-only narration.#Incorrect. The line includes inner rehearsal and judgment.
=D. Second person.#Correct. The narration addresses the character as "you."
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q03::Passage: Scene purpose: show that a character is hiding guilt. Which detail best supports the purpose?{
=A. The character wipes fingerprints from a glass after saying nothing happened.#Correct. The action contradicts the claim and suggests guilt.
~B. The character buys a sandwich.#Incorrect. This does not show guilt unless connected to the conflict.
~C. The character describes a sunny sky.#Incorrect. Weather alone does not show hidden guilt.
~D. The character reads a book title aloud.#Incorrect. This is not clearly connected to guilt.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q04::Passage: "The hallway seemed to narrow as I walked toward the audition room." What does the setting detail do?{
~A. It proves the building is changing shape literally.#Incorrect. The word "seemed" points to perception.
~B. It removes the audition conflict.#Incorrect. It intensifies the conflict.
=C. It mirrors the narrator's anxiety.#Correct. The narrowing hallway shows how pressure affects perception.
~D. It creates an unrelated science lesson.#Incorrect. The focus is emotional experience.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q05::Passage: A student begins a tense scene with four paragraphs of unrelated history. What is the best pacing advice?{
=A. Start closer to the tense moment and include only needed background.#Correct. This helps readers experience the scene's pressure directly.
~B. Keep delaying the tense moment.#Incorrect. Too much unrelated delay weakens tension.
~C. Add more unrelated history.#Incorrect. This increases the pacing problem.
~D. Remove the tense moment.#Incorrect. The tense moment is the scene's center.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q06::Passage: "The empty birdcage hung by the window, door still open." What can the object suggest?{
~A. A complete recipe.#Incorrect. No cooking details appear.
~B. A change to second person.#Incorrect. The object does not create point of view.
~C. No possible meaning.#Incorrect. The detail is emphasized and meaningful.
=D. Loss, escape, or absence in the scene.#Correct. The empty open cage is a focused detail readers can interpret.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q07::Passage: "Mae said she was ready, but she folded the permission slip smaller and smaller." What does the contrast show?{
~A. Mae has already submitted the slip.#Incorrect. She is still holding and folding it.
=B. Mae's actions complicate her spoken claim.#Correct. Her nervous action suggests she may not feel ready.
~C. Mae is not part of the scene.#Incorrect. Mae is the central character.
~D. Mae is giving a weather report.#Incorrect. No weather appears.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q08::Passage: Which statement best describes third-person limited narration?{
~A. It uses "I" and only the narrator's voice.#Incorrect. That is first person.
~B. It uses "you" to address the character.#Incorrect. That is second person.
=C. It uses third-person pronouns while staying close to one character's experience.#Correct. Third-person limited follows one character closely.
~D. It always reveals every character's thoughts.#Incorrect. That is closer to omniscient narration.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q09::Passage: "The trophy shelf was full, but Arin stared at the empty hook where the team jacket used to hang." What is emphasized?{
~A. Arin has never won anything.#Incorrect. The full trophy shelf suggests wins.
~B. The scene is about cleaning shelves.#Incorrect. The missing jacket creates emotional focus.
~C. The hook is unrelated.#Incorrect. It is the detail Arin stares at.
=D. The missing jacket matters more than the visible trophies in this moment.#Correct. The contrast directs attention to absence.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q10::Passage: A scene's purpose is to show friendship becoming strained. Which choice best fits?{
=A. Two friends avoid looking at the friendship bracelet on the desk.#Correct. The shared object and avoidance reveal strain.
~B. Two strangers discuss a recipe with no conflict.#Incorrect. This does not show friendship strain.
~C. A narrator lists every classroom rule.#Incorrect. Rules do not show the relationship problem.
~D. A character describes shoes unrelated to the friend.#Incorrect. The detail does not support the purpose.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q11::Passage: "The narrator says the storm did not scare her, then sleeps with every light on." What should a reader infer?{
~A. She controls the storm.#Incorrect. No evidence supports that.
~B. The storm is unrelated to mood.#Incorrect. The storm and lights work together.
=C. Her behavior contradicts her claim.#Correct. Sleeping with every light on suggests fear.
~D. She is definitely fearless.#Incorrect. Her action complicates that claim.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q12::Passage: "The old key would not open the door, but I kept it on the ring." What craft question is most useful?{
~A. How many keys exist in the world?#Incorrect. That does not help the scene.
=B. Why does the narrator keep a key that no longer works?#Correct. This asks how the object reveals character or memory.
~C. What is the dictionary definition of metal?#Incorrect. Definition does not develop narrative meaning.
~D. Can the sentence be made unrelated?#Incorrect. Revision should strengthen connection.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q13::Passage: Which option best shows a turning point?{
=A. After avoiding the letter all week, the character opens it in front of everyone.#Correct. The action changes the scene's direction.
~B. The character walks past a mailbox with no reaction.#Incorrect. Nothing changes.
~C. The narrator lists the colors of envelopes.#Incorrect. This is description without a clear shift.
~D. The scene ends before any choice occurs.#Incorrect. A turning point needs change or decision.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q14::Passage: "The classroom clock clicked after Ren admitted he copied the poem." What does the sound detail do?{
~A. It proves Ren is innocent.#Incorrect. Ren admitted copying.
~B. It changes the setting to a kitchen.#Incorrect. The passage says classroom.
~C. It removes consequence.#Incorrect. It heightens consequence.
=D. It stretches the silence after confession.#Correct. The clock makes the pause feel tense and noticeable.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q15::Passage: A writer wants to revise vague telling into showing. Which revision is strongest?{
~A. "She was angry."#Incorrect. This tells the emotion directly.
~B. "She was very angry."#Incorrect. Adding "very" still tells.
=C. "She smiled while twisting the napkin until it tore."#Correct. The action lets readers infer controlled anger.
~D. "Angry means mad."#Incorrect. Definition does not create a scene.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q16::Passage: "Every time Nolan told the story, the broken window moved farther from his own baseball bat." What is suggested?{
~A. Nolan is giving exact facts.#Incorrect. The changing story suggests the opposite.
=B. Nolan may be unreliable or avoiding responsibility.#Correct. Changing the detail around his bat affects trust.
~C. The window has no role.#Incorrect. It is the key evidence.
~D. The scene is only about sports rules.#Incorrect. It is about narration and responsibility.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q17::Passage: Which sentence best integrates character and setting?{
~A. The room was a room.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not develop character.
~B. A building existed somewhere.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. The weather was weather.#Incorrect. This gives no meaningful scene detail.
=D. Sal traced the courthouse steps with his shoe before walking inside.#Correct. Setting and action work together to show hesitation before a serious moment.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q18::Passage: "I laughed when my name was called, but the sound came out sharp enough to make Mom turn." What does first person reveal?{
=A. The narrator's awareness of how the laugh sounded.#Correct. First person gives access to the narrator's perception of the moment.
~B. Every thought Mom has.#Incorrect. The narrator only observes Mom turning.
~C. A complete history of the event.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on one moment.
~D. No emotion.#Incorrect. The strange laugh suggests emotion.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q19::Passage: Scene purpose: show a character accepting change. Which detail supports it best?{
~A. The character denies the move is happening.#Incorrect. Denial does not show acceptance.
=B. The character labels the last moving box and leaves the porch light on for the new family.#Correct. The action shows closure and care.
~C. The character hides all the boxes.#Incorrect. Hiding suggests avoidance.
~D. The character lists unrelated snacks.#Incorrect. This does not support accepting change.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q20::Passage: "The candle on the windowsill went out just as the argument stopped." What is the strongest effect?{
~A. The candle explains math.#Incorrect. No math appears.
~B. The argument is unrelated to the candle.#Incorrect. Timing connects them.
=C. The setting detail reinforces the sudden end of tension.#Correct. The extinguished candle echoes the stopped argument.
~D. The passage becomes a recipe.#Incorrect. It remains narrative.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q21::Passage: Which planning step should come first when building an integrated scene?{
=A. Decide what change, pressure, or realization the scene should show.#Correct. Purpose guides point of view, detail, pacing, and revision.
~B. Add random objects before knowing the purpose.#Incorrect. Random objects can weaken focus.
~C. Choose the longest sentence possible.#Incorrect. Length is not the main goal.
~D. Avoid deciding what the scene means.#Incorrect. Meaning is needed for integration.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q22::Passage: "The suitcase was light because I had packed only the things I could explain." What does this line reveal?{
~A. The suitcase is definitely empty.#Incorrect. It contains some things.
~B. The narrator is not leaving.#Incorrect. A packed suitcase suggests departure.
=C. The narrator is choosing objects carefully because leaving has social or emotional stakes.#Correct. "Could explain" suggests pressure around what the objects mean.
~D. The sentence has no character information.#Incorrect. The packing choice reveals the narrator.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q23::Passage: A student's scene has good dialogue but no action, setting, or key details. What feedback is best?{
~A. Delete the conflict.#Incorrect. Conflict is needed.
~B. Make every speaker sound identical.#Incorrect. That weakens characterization.
~C. Add unrelated facts about history.#Incorrect. Unrelated information does not solve missing scene detail.
=D. Add purposeful action and setting details that show what is at stake.#Correct. Dialogue works better when readers can see pressure and response.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q24::Passage: "The narrator knows what every person in the stadium thinks at the same time." Which point of view does this most resemble?{
~A. First person limited.#Incorrect. First person limited would not know every mind.
=B. Third-person omniscient.#Correct. Omniscient narration can access many characters' thoughts.
~C. Second person.#Incorrect. Second person uses "you."
~D. A text message only.#Incorrect. The description is about narrative perspective.
}
::CW_U02_PRE_Q25::Passage: Which revision question best checks Unit 2 readiness?{
=A. Do the narrator, character actions, setting details, pacing, and key objects work together to create one clear effect?#Correct. This checks the main Unit 2 narrative craft skills.
~B. Did I avoid all concrete details?#Incorrect. Purposeful details are needed.
~C. Did I make the scene as confusing as possible?#Incorrect. The goal is meaningful craft, not confusion.
~D. Did I ignore point of view?#Incorrect. Point of view is central to Unit 2.
}
